Subject: Shortest 16 and 17 digit doubles in exact 128-bit integer arithmetic

What this does
  - `dtoa_exact_nd`: for a non-integral normal double a = m * 2^q below 2^53, the decimal of exactly nd significant digits in a's rounding interval nearest to a (ties to even), from T = 4m * 10^k in unsigned 128-bit integers: floor(a * 10^k) is T >> (2 - q) and the remainder gives the distances to it and to the next integer
  - `js_dtoa_short` (Number.prototype.toString and JSON.stringify, 1e-6 <= |d| < 1e15): tries 15 digits exactly first; when there is no 15-digit decimal it returns the 16-, else 17-digit one, skipping the float loop and the multi-precision `js_dtoa`; otherwise the existing float loop runs as before
  - `dtoa_fixed_out`: the fixed-notation writer, shared

Why we need it
  Doubles from arithmetic usually need 16-17 digits; each went through up to 16 failed float tries and then js_dtoa's multi-precision path, ~23% of stringify cycles on the app-traffic benchmark. json_app stringify only: -8.5% cycles, -9.3% instructions.

Soundness
  A decimal of n digits in the interval gives one of n + 1 (append a zero), so the shortest length is the first n with one, and 15 failing means at least 16. All arithmetic is exact: 4m < 2^55, 10^k <= 10^21 < 2^70, S = 2 - q <= 120, so T < 2^125; k is chosen so floor(a * 10^k) has nd digits and corrected exactly. The interval is [4m - lo, 4m + 2] in units of 2^(q-2), lo = 1 at a power of two (half gap below), closed when m is even (round-half-even maps the ends to a). Nearest wins, ties to even R. A carry to nd + 1 digits, k outside 0..21 or no __int128 return to the existing paths. Differentials identical old vs new: new jdt2.js (3,300,481 values: random bit patterns 2^-26..2^53, powers of two and their neighbours, neighbours of 1e-7..1e15, 14-17 digit decimals scaled and their neighbours, quotients; String() and JSON.stringify; all round-trip), jdtoa (1.5M), jstr, jprim, jnum.

